I don't look up from the ward ledgers. The ledgers are open to a page I have already read four times this week. I am looking for inconsistencies in the steward's hand. I have not yet found any. That is its own kind of finding.

"Thank you," I say. "I am occupied."

The steward does not leave. He stands in the doorway of the archive, a figure drawn with the precise, certain lines of a political border. "The Ashmark was... specific in his request."

"The Ashmark is specific in all his requests. That is what makes them requests and not suggestions."

A pause. The frost behind my left eye gives the faintest twitch—not a prick, but the memory of one. The steward is choosing his next words carefully. Men who choose their words carefully are either about to lie or about to reveal a truth they thought they had hidden.

"The court's annual census is complete," he says. "Perhaps you would like to review the records. To familiarize yourself with the... holdings."

I set down the pen. Look up. The steward is a man of middling years, middling height, middling in every dimension except the precision of his courtesy. His face is a map of careful omissions.

"The court's census," I repeat. "How many souls reside in the Withered Court?"

"Souls is an imprecise term, my lady."

"How many people, then?"

"Seven hundred and forty-three. Down from nine hundred and twelve at the last count."

The frost gives a harder twitch. "And the count before that?"

"Twelve hundred and six."

I lean back in my chair. "The court is losing population at an accelerating rate. Two hundred souls in one generation. One hundred and sixty the generation before. Where are they going?"

The steward's face does not change. "Some return to the mortal lands. Some... are lost to the Mire."

"The Mire does not take people, Steward. Not unless they are pushed."

The frost is a needle now. A small one, but sharp. The steward's mask of courtesy does not slip, but something behind it shifts. A door closing. A wall going up.

"I will convey your regrets to the Ashmark," he says, and withdraws.

I sit for a long time after he leaves, staring at the ledgers without reading them. The numbers are a map of a slow catastrophe. A court that loses two hundred souls in a generation is not dying the way a body dies—it is dying the way a language dies. One word at a time, until there is no one left to speak it.

I turn to a fresh page in my journal and write a single line: *Where did they go?*

It is the first question of the real survey. Not the map of the court's walls or its wards or its ley-lines, but the map of its absences. The negative space where people used to be.

The frost behind my eye is quiet.

But the question is not.

## III.

The second knock comes at mid-afternoon. Two knocks, same rhythm, but sharper. Urgent.

I open the door to Mira. Her face is the face she wears when she has been carrying something heavy for too long—not physical weight, but the kind that presses on the sternum.

"The Ashmark sent me," she says. "There is something you should see."

"I am—"

"Working. I know. This is not work. This is the thing that stops the work from mattering."

She turns and walks. I follow, through the corridors I have begun to map, down stairs I have not yetcatalogued, into the deep foundations where the stone is older and the air tastes of iron and forgotten centuries.

We reach a door I have not seen before. It is small, iron-banded, set into the wall at an angle that suggests it was built to be overlooked. Mira produces a key—not the court's standard issue, but something older, heavier, its teeth worn smooth from use.

"Where does this lead?" I ask.

"Nowhere on any chart you have drawn."

She unlocks the door. The hinges make no sound—recently oiled. Beyond the door, a narrow stair descends into darkness. The walls are wet. The air smells of old vellum and chemical ink and something else—something sharp and metallic that I recognize from the north country. Iron-gall. The ink of official records.

We descend. The stair is steep, the steps uneven. I keep one hand on the wall, reading the stone. It is older than the court above. Older than the Sundering, perhaps. The marks on the walls are not Thornscript—they are something else, something that predates the language of names.

At the bottom, a chamber.

It is not large—perhaps twenty feet square. But every surface is covered in maps. Not painted. Not drawn. *Carved*. Into the stone itself, in lines so fine they could have been made by a needle.

I step closer. The maps are not of the Withered Court. They are of something else—a territory I do not recognize, with symbols I cannot read. And yet there is something familiar in the contours, in the way the lines bend and branch, in the rhythm of the spacing.

"This is—" I begin.

"A map of the Mire," Mira says. "But not the Mire as it is now. The Mire as it was. Before the Sundering. Before the Thornborn. Before names."

I run my fingers over the carved lines. They are precise, mathematical, the work of someone who understood the territory at a level I have never achieved. This is not a survey. This is a *memory*.

"Who made this?"

"The First Warden. The first lord of this court. Before he had a name, before he was the Ashmark, before any of it."

"But why carve it into the stone? Why hide it down here?"

Mira's face is grim in the lamplight. "Because the map is not just a record. It is a *key*. The ley-lines respond to it. The wards are built on it. Every ward-stone in the court corresponds to a line on this map."

I stare at the carved walls. "Who else knows about this room?"

"The First Warden. The Ashmark. And now us."

I turn to her. "The Ashmark does not know we are here."

"No." She meets my eyes. "He does not."

The frost behind my eye is silent. The room itself seems to hold its breath. I look at the maps, at the carved lines that predate everything I have been taught about this court. The ley-lines I have been charting, the wards I have been studying, the very geometry of the territory—it all begins *here*.

In a room no one is supposed to find.

Behind a door no one is supposed to open.

"Why are you showing me this?" I ask.

Mira is quiet for a long time. Then: "Because you are the only one who will ask what the map means, instead of what it can be used for."
